usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Usage:
  atyrode apply [HOST] [--ref REF] [--repo PATH] [--git-auth-mode ssh|https-gh] [--plan|--dry-run|--preview-json] [--json] [--restart-shell]
  atyrode apply-status [JOB] [--json]
  atyrode capabilities list [--json]
  atyrode capabilities show [HOST] [--json]
  atyrode runtime list [--json]
  atyrode runtime status [local-qwen|manifold-agent] [--json]
  atyrode runtime provision local-qwen [--data-dir PATH] [--yes]
  atyrode runtime provision manifold-agent [--rotate-token]
  atyrode runtime start|stop|run [local-qwen]
  atyrode runtime start|stop|restart manifold-agent
  atyrode runtime autostart [local-qwen] on|off
  atyrode runtime boot
  atyrode runtime shortcut [local-qwen]
  atyrode inventory [--host HOST] [--ref REF | --repo PATH] --json
  atyrode infra setup|plan|apply [--repo PATH] [--json] [--yes]
  atyrode vault status [--json]
  atyrode vault login|lock
  atyrode vault get NAME
  atyrode vault put NAME
  atyrode auth broker publish --via USER@HOST
  atyrode auth broker setup
  atyrode auth broker status [--json]
  atyrode auth broker add-api-key PROVIDER
  atyrode tunnel list [--json]
  atyrode tunnel grant NAME [--for 1h|8h|24h|7d|until-revoked]
  atyrode tunnel revoke NAME
  atyrode provision git [--persist] [--yes]
  atyrode provision babel [--dry-run]
  atyrode lifecycle [--json]
  atyrode doctor [HOST] [--json]
  atyrode doctor host [HOST] [--json]
  atyrode doctor system [HOST] [--json]
  atyrode doctor git [--json]
  atyrode doctor tools [--json]
  atyrode doctor provisioning [--json]
  atyrode host [HOST] [--json]
  atyrode generations [--json] [--sizes]
  atyrode rollback [--to N] [--dry-run] [--yes]
  atyrode clean [--dry-run] [--keep N] [--keep-since DUR] [--all] [--yes] [--verbose] [--json]
  atyrode windows plan|apply [HOST] [--json]

Fixed hosts keep registry-owned identity; portable bootstrap profiles bind the
validated invoking user and HOME at activation time. apply activates the
published flake by default, resolving REF (default main) to an exact commit
first; --repo switches to a local checkout for development.
apply preview modes are non-activating: --plan resolves and validates the target
then prints preflight metadata without invoking nh; --dry-run invokes the normal
nh switch backend with --dry; --preview-json runs that dry backend and emits its
normalized package/action preview as JSON.
apply validates everything before invoking nh, and afterwards reports plain-omp
settings that drifted from the seeded defaults. Mutating Linux Home Manager
applies run as manager-owned jobs rather than as children of the invoking
shell. Started from a terminal the job is handed that terminal: activation
output streams as it happens, every prompt it raises (sudo, the vault) is
answerable, and the job ends with the terminal. Without one it is detached and
its output captured, so replacing a terminal-hosting service cannot terminate
the activation and apply-status reconnects to the durable result. On a terminal
apply also offers an interactive keep-or-reset review after activation, and
offers to run the provisioning command for each surface it finds unconfigured
instead of only naming it.
inventory evaluates the versioned manifest at this CLI's exact revision by
default; --ref selects another published revision and --repo evaluates a local
checkout. It is read-only and currently requires --json.
infra provides the portable controller for the externally owned tyrode-dev-01
NixOS host. setup performs one-time Clan age enrollment, plan verifies the
Bitwarden identity, pinned infra checkout, Vars state, and strict SSH path
without activation. apply fetches origin/main, visibly fast-forwards a clean
main checkout, refuses local or divergent commits, and deploys through Clan only
after showing the revision and incoming commits for confirmation. The private age
identity is read only after an interactive Bitwarden unlock, materialized in
temporary storage, never printed, and removed on every exit.
vault provides exact-name Bitwarden Secure Note storage. get and put sync and
unlock interactively when needed, then relock only when they opened the session.
put accepts secret material only from hidden terminal input or stdin; get is the
explicit secret-output boundary. Duplicate names and non-note items are refused.
tunnel decides which reviewed fleet keys (home/ssh-fleet-keys) may reach THIS
machine over SSH. It owns ~/.ssh/authorized_keys: every mutation re-renders the
file from the registry plus local grant state, keeping the pre-management file
once. Grants default to a timed grant whose deadline is an OpenSSH expiry-time
option, so sshd stops accepting the key on its own with no timer or daemon.
grant and revoke require an unlocked Bitwarden vault as an intentionality gate;
list is read-only and needs no vault. The registry's one primary key is always
rendered and can never be revoked here. Grant state is machine-local and is not
part of a Nix generation or rollback.
lifecycle inventories a fixed set of Home Manager, Git/OMP worktree, and OMP/atyrode
cache/state paths under the configured home. It is read-only, includes OMP's
supported GC/worktree-clear dry-runs when omp is available, and --json emits a stable report.
doctor git is a read-only audit of SSH authentication, SSH signing, forge
remotes, Git credential helpers, and GitHub CLI credential storage. It reports
classifications only and never prints keys, tokens, helper arguments, or remotes.
generations lists the activation profile's generations (nix-darwin or NixOS
system profile, otherwise standalone Home Manager); rollback activates an earlier
one (previous by default, or --to N) after confirming.
clean reclaims disk from generations the
config no longer references, always keeping the current one and a rollback
window (--keep / --keep-since); it previews the plan and asks before touching
anything (--yes skips the prompt, --dry-run only previews), folds nh's verbose
evaluation plan into its own summary (--verbose passes nh's full output through),
and on macOS it also sweeps stale Home Manager Apps aliases. The auto GC roots that pin old
generations are owned by the Nix daemon; a non-root clean cannot unlink them and
prints the exact `sudo … nix-store --gc` command to reap them (atyrode never
self-elevates). Commands reserved for later issues: workspace and agent.
windows treats WinGet as the native Windows package owner. plan is read-only;
apply reconciles exact package IDs through WSL interop after refusing channel
conflicts. Windows mutations are not part of a Nix generation or rollback.
EOF
}

main() {
  if [[ $# == 0 ]] && interactive; then
    export ATYRODE_CLI="$0"
    exec "$atyrode_tui"
  fi
  local subcommand="${1:-help}"
  shift || true
  # A production binary must never mutate the store while a caller believes its
  # tool overrides are in effect (they are not) — refuse before dispatching.
  case "$subcommand" in
    apply | clean | rollback | provision) guard_production_mutation "$subcommand" ;;
  esac
  case "$subcommand" in
    apply) apply_config "$@" ;;
    apply-status) cmd_apply_status "$@" ;;
    __apply-job) run_apply_job_worker "$@" ;;
    clean) cmd_clean "$@" ;;
    generations) cmd_generations "$@" ;;
    lifecycle) cmd_lifecycle "$@" ;;
    rollback) cmd_rollback "$@" ;;
    runtime) cmd_runtime "$@" ;;
    infra) cmd_infra "$@" ;;
    vault) cmd_vault "$@" ;;
    tunnel)
      case "${1:-}" in
        grant | revoke) guard_production_mutation "tunnel $1" ;;
      esac
      cmd_tunnel "$@"
      ;;
    auth) cmd_auth "$@" ;;
    provision) cmd_provision "$@" ;;
    capabilities) capabilities "$@" ;;
    doctor)
      case "${1:-}" in
        host) shift; local requested="" json=0; if [[ $# -gt 0 && "$1" != --json ]]; then requested="$1"; shift; fi; [[ "${1:-}" != --json ]] || json=1; doctor_host "$requested" "$json" ;;
        system) shift; doctor_system "$@" ;;
        git) shift; doctor_git "$@" ;;
        tools) shift; doctor_tools "$@" ;;
        provisioning) shift; doctor_provisioning "$@" ;;
        # No subcommand asks the whole question -- "what is missing on this
        # machine" -- which is the one an operator actually has. Anything that
        # is not a family name is a host for the aggregate; resolve_host names
        # the valid ones, so a mistyped family lands on a useful error.
        *) doctor_all "$@" ;;
      esac
      ;;
    host) local requested="" json=0; if [[ $# -gt 0 && "$1" != --json ]]; then requested="$1"; shift; fi; [[ "${1:-}" != --json ]] || json=1; doctor_host "$requested" "$json" ;;
    inventory) inventory "$@" ;;
    windows) [[ "${1:-}" != apply ]] || guard_production_mutation "windows apply"; cmd_windows "$@" ;;
    workspace|agent) die "$EX_UNAVAILABLE" "$subcommand is reserved for a follow-up issue" ;;
    help|-h|--help) usage ;;
    *) die "$EX_USAGE" "unknown command: $subcommand" ;;
  esac
}

main "$@"
